#
# PROBLEM: quoted strings cross two different worlds. The lexical
# table says "..." is guest-boundary text OR a host path — and that
# "or" is the mush: the same spelling is host-side in one verb and
# guest-side in the next, with the direction of travel invisible.

description "One quoting, two worlds"
platform    dos
machine     stopped
timeout     2m

stage "payloads/AUTOTEST.EXE"   # HOST path — resolved against the
                                # script's directory, forward slashes
start
wait  "AUTOTEST.EXE"            # GUEST text — matched on the screen
enter "AUTOTEST.EXE"            # GUEST text — typed at the prompt
wait  machine=stopped

# One line, two worlds:
collect "RESULTS.LOG" to="results/"
#        ^^^^^^^^^^^^ guest-side file name on the exchange drive
#                        ^^^^^^^^^^ host-side directory under the
#                                   run's output root

# Nothing marks which side a string lives on except the verb's
# signature — and the two sides even prefer different slash
# conventions (host paths forward, guest DOS paths backward):
#
#     stage "payloads/AUTOTEST.EXE"     host, forward slashes
#     enter "C:\PAYLOADS\AUTOTEST.EXE"  guest, backslashes
#
# The redesign fixed this problem for NAMES (@ marks a library
# ref, $ marks an input) but left paths sharing the guest-text
# spelling.
#
# OPEN QUESTION: do host paths deserve their own spelling — a
# sigil-prefixed string, or reusing @ for host artifacts — or is
# per-verb knowledge acceptable because only stage/collect (and
# collect's to=) ever take host paths? The small, closed set of
# path positions argues for the status quo; the two-worlds-in-one-
# line collect argues against.
